 to get a  lot done  i'm sure that my recovery will allow me  to i think i will be a different person  than i was before  but that's okay  peggy green has since gone back to her  normal routine  but she still feels the lingering  effects of illness she often feels weak  and her muscles hurt but even if the  road to recovery is slow  she's glad to be on it  [Music]  dr david strain heads up the british  medical association's work on the  long-term impact of covert and is also a  survivor  we'll get to that in a moment first  david is there any way of speeding up  recovery for  long-term sufferers like the woman in  that report  that's a really interesting question  because we don't really know what the  natural history  of covid itself is of the lunk over it  is so i get many reports of people who  for example are taking multivitamins or  are taking  other health supplements and swerve it  has made a dramatic difference for them  but i also get reports from people  who've done no intervention  and have got dramatically better for no  apparent reason  whereas in all cases we get people who  don't get better so at this moment in  time  in the absence of randomized controlled  trials we don't have  any definitive answer as to what will or  won't work  well take us back to the beginning what  causes long covert in the first place  that in itself is a huge question that  may help us to identify what the  treatments are but  at the moment we don't fully understand  there are two or three different  hypotheses that are being run the first  and the one the  reason that i'm researching into is  looking at the mitochondria that is the  powerhouse of the cells  um effectively if you want to think of  them as the battery that powers your  body  basically we know covid the disease does  attack that battery  and what we believe is happening is that  there's a many individuals who that  battery just isn't recharging after the  diseases  it's like having the old iphone that it  doesn't matter how long you plug it in  for  it will not get the charge that it  requires and we think that's what's  going on  um and that's the theory we're working  on there are  others that think that this is an  autoimmune disease the antibodies you  create  to the virus then turn on the host and  effectively become like  rheumatoid arthritis or sle and trigger  an autoimmune disease  there are some that think there are  permanent scarring that takes place in  the lung  and the blood vessels that occur as a  result of the virus  the bottom line is at the moment we  don't know and  reality is it's likely to be a  combination of  multiple different causes what about  just how many people  this actually affects what do you make  of the chinese study that shows  two-thirds of hard-hit patients suffer  long-term effects  when you're looking at the hard hit it's  no surprise at all if you look at people  who've been on itu for other reasons  many of those will still have long-term  effects six or even 12 months later  i think what's more interesting in long  covered is the number of people like was  described there that  only got relatively mild disease i mean  for me personally i only had  one day that i needed any additional  therapy yet i've been left with symptoms  12 weeks later that are actually worse  than the initial disease  and and these are the ones that are  causing more issues  from the ons the office of national  statistics in the uk  they estimate that 20 of people who had  any symptoms at all are left with  some uh debilitating illness at six  weeks  and ten percent of people even though  they didn't require hospitalization or  any additional care  at the outset ten percent of people  who've ever tested positive  will have symptoms of long covey at  three months  and you've said this is happening to  younger people more women than men  uh those suggested to be at lower risk  exactly and that's part of the thing  that we're seeing particularly in their  um health care setting that  the risk of dying from covid is much  lower in females it's much lower in the  youngsters and therefore  in many healthcare settings these are  the people that we get to work the front  line because  if they catch covid the consequences  won't be as severe  but now what we're seeing is an after  effect is that these are  exactly the people that are more at risk  of long covert  in our study we've identified that even  though women are more likely to  self-present and self-participate in  study  even after we are just for that 86  percent of the long-covered that we're  seeing  is in younger fitter ladies and  and it does appear to be very similar  that all of the people who are coming  presenting with  symptoms of long covert were previously  very fit they were very active  we're not sure if that represents that  they have a higher baseline and  therefore  they they realize it a lot more if  they've been left with  some weakness or it may just be that  these are the people that are actually  noticing it because while most of us  around the world are in some form of  lockdown where we don't have  the same freedoms that we have normally  that we just don't notice how much that  this has had impact on us  just briefly you've also said this isn't  just a respiratory  infection it's a multi-system disease  are there steps that can be taken to  prevent long covert from setting in  we don't really know what the cause is  so it's very difficult to say for  certain but  one thing that we are trialling and  having good responsive with  is making sure that when you've got the  illness  you stay within your exercise envelope  or your energy envelope we call it  um for many other people training if  you're training for a marathon or if  you're  training for a race you want to push  yourself every single time  you train with long covid what we  believe  is if you stay well within your energy  window  then the window itself will get bigger  over time  rather than us trying to push because  one of the key things that all of us are  seeing  is the harder you push yourself the  longer it takes to recover  after each episode so stay within  your energy envelope at every available  opportunity  david strain thank you very much for  being on the show  you're welcome officials are  distributing madonna's coronavirus  vaccine in germany  it's being administered from tuesday and  is the second vaccine available here  still people won't have a choice they'll  have to take whatever comes  germany is facing criticism for a slow  start to its vaccination program  covert infection rates remain high  despite an ongoing knockdown  time for your questions now over to our  science correspondent derek williams  what's the difference between quarantine  and isolation  and what are the correct ways to observe  both  in the course of this pandemic these two  terms have been used interchangeably  quite often although they actually  mean different things and the confusion  arises because  both are protective measures for  stopping potential covet 19 spreaders  from transmitting the disease to others  so  definitions first isolation is defined  as a measure for keeping people known to  be sick with a contagious disease  away from people who don't have it  quarantine  on the other hand is restricting the  movement of people who  might have been exposed to an infectious  disease for a while  to find out whether they've been  infected or not um so  so both are waiting games the big  difference is  that if you have coveted 19 you're in  isolation  if you only might have gotten it you're  in quarantine  when it comes to the right ways to  observe them things get a little  trickier because different health  authorities and different countries  regulate the two in different ways here  in germany for example  if you've tested positive for covet 19  and are recovering at home  you have to isolate for at least 10 days  from either the day you tested positive  or  the day you began to show symptoms  though you can also  only leave isolation if a doctor  determines that your symptoms have  really improved and remained that way  for at least 48 hours  those who have been directly exposed to  someone with the disease for example  family members they're what are called  category one contacts and they have to  quarantine  and germany's health authority says they  should do so after their last  contact with the sick person for two  full weeks  um although they can also leave  quarantine if they test negative for  covet 19  after 10 days

